Cabinet-Gong Skronk

Ingredients: FUSION / JAZZ ROCK + EUPHORIC, ENERGETIC & JOLLY + Cymbals
Creative Catalyst: Teenage drift-kids hijacked an abandoned mall's dead arcade, wiring cabinet motherboards into brass percussion to unleash a hyper-kinetic brass-and-byte carnival.

Cabinet-Gong Skronk is a blindingly bright, hyper-virtuosic collision of complex 1970s jazz-rock dexterity and unhinged, carnival-esque euphoria. It sounds like a 16-bit arcade processor suffering a heat-stroke inside a room filled entirely with spun-bronze cymbals and racing electric basslines. Atonal, rapid-fire horn charts collide with hyper-cranked, polyrhythmic metallic wash, creating a dizzying sensory overload that feels impossibly joyful, violently optimistic, and physically relentless.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 148–172 BPM. Hyper-syncopated 13/8 and 7/4 time signatures that feel like a tumbling freight train of brass, constantly resolving into wildly triumphant, four-on-the-floor jazz-fusion breakdowns.
*   Harmonic Palette: Lydian Dominant and Super-Locrian scales played at breakneck speed, colliding with bright, major-seven add-nine chord stacks and microtonal 8-bit frequency-glide intervals.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Cymbal-Exciter Saturation: Splicing high-frequency cymbal overheads through overdriven arcade-board preamps, causing metal hits to spit literal sparks of bit-crushed noise.
    2. Logic-Gate Gating: Ducking the entire rhythm section against the erratic, live-triggered contact-mikes glued to vintage arcade joysticks.
    3. Hyper-Resonant Room Bleed: Recording horn sections inside dead concrete escalators to create a wet, metallic echo that bounces wildly across the stereo field.
*   Dynamics: Relentlessly explosive; tracks start at maximum velocity and somehow scale upwards into blinding peaks of brass and metal, dipping only for frantic, microtonal bass solos.

